WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:04.880
Seed Stitch and Moss Stitch are very popular

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.480
in the handknitting world.
They are reversible and highly textured.

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.480
These stitches can be problematic
for machine knitters since our machines

4
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:20.040
don't form both knit
and purl stitches easily.

5
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.840
Seed Stich alternates knit and purl stitches every row.

6
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.360
Moss stitch alternates knit
and purl stitches every other row.

7
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.160
If you are lucky enough to have a brother
standard gauge machine with a G-carriage,

8
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.360
the turtle can make quick
work of the stitch pattern.

9
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:48.240
For small sections,
hand manipulation is an option.

10
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.560
For Seed Stitch, reform

11
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:57.120
every other needle, every other
row alternating the stitches.

12
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.880
For Moss Sttich reform every other needle,

13
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.800
every two rows,
alternating every two rows.

14
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.320
If you have a Ribber,
you might consider transferring the Seed

15
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.560
or Moss Stitches between the beds
to form the knit and purl stitches.

16
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.040
Either way, when you're hand manipulating

17
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.640
this type of stitch pattern,
it can be time-consuming,

18
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.120
but given in small doses,
it can be well worth the effort.

